   "op": "replace_brief",
   "brief": "limp",
   "html": "<div class=\"brief sp\" id=\"limp\" data-entry=\"hub\" data-lens=\"peds\" data-spine=\"2\" data-shelf=\"fm peds\" data-bp=\"msk\">\n<h4>The Limping Child</h4>\n<p class=\"sub\">sick or well first &middot; age at onset &middot; time course &middot; the hip exam &middot; imaging by the findings &middot; treatment by cause</p>\n<nav class=\"sp-step\" aria-label=\"Decision spine\"><a href=\"#lm-s1\"><b>1</b><span>Presentation</span></a><a href=\"#lm-s2\"><b>2</b><span>Urgent</span></a><a href=\"#lm-dx\"><b>3</b><span>Differential</span></a><a href=\"#lm-s4\"><b>4</b><span>Workup</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>5</b><span>Criteria</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>6</b><span>Severity</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>7</b><span>Treatment</span></a><a class=\"off\" aria-disabled=\"true\" title=\"Not used in this brief\"><b>8</b><span>Follow-up</span></a><a href=\"#lp-bank\" class=\"sp-prac\"><span>Practice</span></a></nav>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"lm-s1\"><b class=\"sp-n\">1</b>Presentation</h5>\n<div class=\"dp\"><span class=\"lbl\">Bottom line</span><ul class=\"bl\"><li><b>Sick first</b>: fever or refusal to bear weight is <b>septic arthritis</b> until the Kocher criteria and the tap say otherwise</li><li><b>Night pain</b>, bruising or weight loss: <b>CBC with smear</b> for leukemia before calling a limp benign</li><li>Then match the <b>age</b> and the <b>duration</b>: a limp past about <b>2 weeks</b> makes transient synovitis (toxic synovitis) unlikely; <b>weeks to months</b> points to Perthes disease, slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE), juvenile idiopathic arthritis or a tumor</li></ul></div>\n<div class=\"sp-script sp-s2\"><div class=\"sr\"><span class=\"sk\">What it is</span><div class=\"sv\"><p>A child who walks unevenly, with or without pain</p></div></div><div class=\"sr\"><span class=\"sk\">Sort by</span><div class=\"sv\"><p>Sick or well, then age, then duration</p></div></div><div class=\"sr\"><span class=\"sk\">Knee pain</span><div class=\"sv\"><p>With a normal knee exam: examine the hip</p></div></div><div class=\"sr\"><span class=\"sk\">Hands off</span><div class=\"sv\"><p>Infection, the slipped epiphysis, arthritis, leukemia and bone lesions each have their own brief<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"septic-hip\">Septic Arthritis and Transient Synovitis in a Child</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"scfe\">Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"sjia\">Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-leukemia\">Acute Leukemia in a Child</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bone-tumors\">Bone Lesions in a Child</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"growing-pains\">Leg Pain in a Child</button></div></p></div></div></div>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"lm-s2\"><b class=\"sp-n\">2</b>Urgent risks</h5>\n<div class=\"sp-rows\">\n<div class=\"sp-row\"><span class=\"sp-src\">Septic arthritis</span><b class=\"sp-f\">Fever, a toxic child, refuses to bear weight; pain on any hip motion</b><div class=\"sp-chips\"><div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"septic-hip\">Septic Arthritis and Transient Synovitis in a Child</button></div></div><p class=\"sp-commit\"><b>If</b> there is fever or no weight bearing, <b>choose</b> <span class=\"mask\">Kocher criteria and hip US; aspirate before antibiotics</span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"sp-row\"><span class=\"sp-src\">Leukemia</span><b class=\"sp-f\">Night pain that wakes the child, bruising, pallor, weight loss</b><div class=\"sp-chips\"><div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-leukemia\">Acute Leukemia in a Child</button></div></div><p class=\"sp-commit\"><b>If</b> any of these comes with the limp, <b>choose</b> <span class=\"mask\">CBC with smear before anything is called benign</span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"sp-row\"><span class=\"sp-src\">Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (SCFE)</span><b class=\"sp-f\">Age 10 to 15; groin, thigh or knee pain; lost internal rotation</b><div class=\"sp-chips\"><div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"scfe\">Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis</button></div></div><p class=\"sp-commit\"><b>If</b> the radiograph shows a slip, <b>choose</b> <span class=\"mask\">no weight bearing; urgent pinning in situ</span>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"sp-row\"><span class=\"sp-src\">Abuse</span><b class=\"sp-f\">A corner fracture, or an injury the story does not explain</b><div class=\"sp-chips\"><div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-nat-fracture\">Suspected Child Abuse</button></div></div></div>\n</div>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"lm-dx\"><b class=\"sp-n\">3</b>Differential</h5>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>The limping child, most tempting first</caption><thead><tr><th>Diagnosis</th><th>Fingerprint</th><th>Decides it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Transient synovitis <span class=\"aka\">(toxic synovitis)</span><div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"septic-hip\">Septic Arthritis and Transient Synovitis in a Child</button></div></td><td><span>Days after a cold; looks well, walks with a limp; exam, labs and radiograph normal</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Days; gone within about 2 weeks</b>; afebrile, markers normal</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Septic arthritis<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"septic-hip\">Septic Arthritis and Transient Synovitis in a Child</button></div></td><td><span>Fever, toxic, will not bear weight; pain on any motion</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Hours to days</b>; fever with a raised erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) and white cell count</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Osteomyelitis<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"septic-hip\">Septic Arthritis and Transient Synovitis in a Child</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-puncture-osteomyelitis\">Foot Puncture Wounds and Osteomyelitis</button></div></td><td><span>Fever and malaise; point bone tenderness</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Hours to days</b>; marrow signal on MRI</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Legg-Calv&eacute;-Perthes disease <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"lm-pop-1\">Avascular necrosis</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"lm-pop-1\" hidden><span class=\"sp-ct\">Avascular necrosis (osteonecrosis)</span>Legg-Calv&eacute;-Perthes disease and SCFE both cause avascular necrosis (osteonecrosis) of the femoral head; in Perthes disease the blood supply is cut off for no known reason (idiopathic), and the head flattens and fragments before it heals.</span></td><td><span>Boy of 5 to 10, peak 5 to 7, about 4 to 5 times more often than girls; painless limp, then hip, thigh or knee pain</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Weeks to months</b>; abduction and internal rotation lost; flat, fragmented femoral head <button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-pop\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"lm-pop-2\">Early film</button><span class=\"sp-pop-b\" id=\"lm-pop-2\" hidden><span class=\"sp-ct\">Perthes disease on radiographs</span>The first radiograph in Perthes disease is often normal or shows only slight flattening; fragmentation comes later in the course. A normal film with a persistent limp is followed by MRI.</span></span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Slipped capital femoral epiphysis<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"scfe\">Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis</button></div></td><td><span>Age 10 to 15, the growth spurt; often obese; groin, thigh or knee pain</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Weeks to months</b>; internal rotation lost; the hip rotates outward on flexion</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Toddler&#x27;s fracture</td><td><span>Most often 9 months to 3 years; will not bear weight after a trip or fall</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Days</b>; tibial shaft tenderness; spiral fracture of the tibia</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH)</td><td><span>Infant: positive Barlow or Ortolani maneuver; breech, girl, firstborn, family history (the 4 Fs); asymmetric skin folds</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Since walking began</b>; painless limp, short leg, limited abduction</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Juvenile idiopathic arthritis<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"sjia\">Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis</button></div></td><td><span>Swollen joint, morning stiffness that eases with activity</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>6 weeks or more</b></span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Leukemia<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-leukemia\">Acute Leukemia in a Child</button></div></td><td><span>Night pain, bruising, pallor, weight loss</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Weeks to months</b>; cytopenias or blasts on the smear</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Growing pains<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"growing-pains\">Leg Pain in a Child</button></div></td><td><span>Both legs, late in the day or at night; exam normal</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>No limp</b>: growing pains do not cause one</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Bone tumor<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bone-tumors\">Bone Lesions in a Child</button></div></td><td><span>Bone pain worse at night</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>Weeks to months</b>; osteoid osteoma eases with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Acute rheumatic fever<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"arf\">Acute Rheumatic Fever</button></div></td><td><span>Migratory large-joint arthritis</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\"><b>2 to 4 weeks after pharyngitis</b>; timed to the throat, not the joint</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Osgood-Schlatter disease</td><td><span>Adolescent athlete after the growth spurt; pain below the knee</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Tender, prominent tibial tubercle; hip normal</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Psoas abscess or appendicitis</td><td><span>Pain when the hip is extended (psoas sign)</span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">The hip joint itself is normal</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"lcw\"><div class=\"ttl\"><span>Age at onset</span><small>not to scale</small></div><ol class=\"wrows\"><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Developmental dysplasia of the hip</b><span class=\"win\">Birth; a missed one shows once walking</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:20%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:36%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:50%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:72%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c3\" style=\"--a:0%;--b:36%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Newborn</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:20%\">1 yr</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:36%\">3</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:50%\">5</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:72%\">10</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">15 yr</span><em>&rarr;</em></div><p class=\"wl\"><b>Image</b>US under 4 months; radiograph after</p></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Toddler&#x27;s fracture</b><span class=\"win\">9 months to 3 years</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:20%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:36%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:50%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:72%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c1\" style=\"--a:15%;--b:36%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Newborn</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:20%\">1 yr</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:36%\">3</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:50%\">5</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:72%\">10</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">15 yr</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Transient synovitis</b><span class=\"win\">About 3 to 10 years</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:20%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:36%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:50%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:72%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c1\" style=\"--a:36%;--b:72%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Newborn</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:20%\">1 yr</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:36%\">3</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:50%\">5</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:72%\">10</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">15 yr</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Legg-Calv&eacute;-Perthes disease</b><span class=\"win\">5 to 10 years; peak 5 to 7</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:20%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:36%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:50%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:72%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c3\" style=\"--a:50%;--b:72%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Newborn</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:20%\">1 yr</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:36%\">3</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:50%\">5</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:72%\">10</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">15 yr</span><em>&rarr;</em></div><p class=\"wl\"><b>Sex</b>boys about 4 to 1</p></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Slipped capital femoral epiphysis</b><span class=\"win\">Age 10 to 15, in the growth spurt</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:20%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:36%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:50%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:72%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c3\" style=\"--a:72%;--b:95%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Newborn</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:20%\">1 yr</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:36%\">3</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:50%\">5</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:72%\">10</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">15 yr</span><em>&rarr;</em></div><p class=\"wl\"><b>Build</b>any; obesity raises the risk</p></div></li></ol><div class=\"lc-key\"><span><i class=\"k-ac\"></i>Acute: days</span><span><i class=\"k-chr\"></i>Weeks to months</span></div></figure>\n<figure class=\"lcw\"><div class=\"ttl\"><span>Time course</span><small>not to scale</small></div><ol class=\"wrows\"><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Septic arthritis, osteomyelitis</b><span class=\"win\">Hours to days, with fever</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:28%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:44%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:72%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar inf\" style=\"--a:0%;--b:28%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Onset</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:28%\">1 week</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:44%\">2 weeks</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:72%\">6 weeks</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">3 months</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Transient synovitis, toddler&#x27;s fracture</b><span class=\"win\">Days; most synovitis is gone within about 2 weeks</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:28%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:44%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:72%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c1\" style=\"--a:0%;--b:44%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Onset</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:28%\">1 week</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:44%\">2 weeks</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:72%\">6 weeks</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">3 months</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Juvenile idiopathic arthritis</b><span class=\"win\">6 weeks or more; stiff each morning</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:28%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:44%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:72%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c3\" style=\"--a:72%;--b:100%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Onset</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:28%\">1 week</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:44%\">2 weeks</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:72%\">6 weeks</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">3 months</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li><li class=\"wr\"><div class=\"wh\"><b class=\"nm mask\">Perthes disease, SCFE, tumor</b><span class=\"win\">Insidious: weeks to months</span></div><div class=\"wb\"><div class=\"rail\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:0%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:28%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:44%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:72%\"></i><i class=\"tk\" style=\"--x:95%\"></i><i class=\"bar c3 open\" style=\"--a:28%;--b:100%\"></i></div><div class=\"rax\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"a0\" style=\"--x:0%\">Onset</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:28%\">1 week</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:44%\">2 weeks</span><span class=\"\" style=\"--x:72%\">6 weeks</span><span class=\"a1\" style=\"--x:95%\">3 months</span><em>&rarr;</em></div></div></li></ol><div class=\"lc-key\"><span><i class=\"k-inf\"></i>Infection: act today</span><span><i class=\"k-ac\"></i>Acute: days</span><span><i class=\"k-chr\"></i>Weeks to months</span></div></figure>\n<h5 class=\"tsec sp-h\" id=\"lm-s4\"><b class=\"sp-n\">4</b>Workup</h5>\n<figure class=\"lcp w5a\"><p class=\"kick\">Diagnostic workup</p><p class=\"w5z\">First step</p><div class=\"w5t first\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Every limping child</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Temperature and weight bearing</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Fever or no weight bearing</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Septic arthritis: Kocher criteria, hip US, tap</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b w5k\"><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">CBC with smear, erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR) and C-reactive protein (CRP)</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Cytopenias or blasts</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Leukemia</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b w5k\"><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Hip rotation and abduction</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Internal rotation lost</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Hip disease: Perthes disease or SCFE</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Abduction limited</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">DDH</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5z\">Targeted tests</p><div class=\"w5t\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Under 5, well, no fever, nothing localized</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Radiographs of both tibias and fibulas</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Spiral fracture of the tibia</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Toddler&#x27;s fracture</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Corner fracture</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Suspect abuse</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Walker with a painless limp, short leg, limited abduction</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Anteroposterior radiograph of the pelvis; hip US under 4 months</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Shallow acetabulum, head displaced</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Missed DDH</span></div></div><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Limp past about 2 weeks, or internal rotation lost</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">Anteroposterior and frog-leg radiographs of both hips</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Flat, fragmented femoral head</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1\"><span class=\"mask\">Legg-Calv&eacute;-Perthes disease</span></div><div class=\"w5fd fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Slipped epiphysis</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m fl\"><span class=\"mask\">SCFE</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5z\">Next step</p><div class=\"w5t\"><div class=\"w5b\"><div class=\"w5c\">Fever with point bone tenderness, or a normal film and a persistent limp</div><span class=\"w5ar\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;</span><div class=\"w5o\"><span class=\"mask w5om\"><span class=\"w5ol\">MRI</span></span></div></div><div class=\"w5f\"><div class=\"w5fd f1 fl\"><span class=\"t\" lang=\"en\">Marrow signal</span><span class=\"ld\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span></div><span class=\"w5an f1 fl\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></span><div class=\"w5m f1 fl\"><span class=\"mask\">Osteomyelitis, or early Perthes disease</span></div></div></div><p class=\"w5skip\"><span class=\"w5z\">Skip</span><span class=\"mask\">Knee films for knee pain with a normal knee exam: the hip refers pain to the knee. Hip US in an infant past 4 months: the femoral head has ossified.</span></p></figure>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Treatment by cause</caption><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Treatment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>DDH</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Under 6 months: Pavlik harness, hips flexed and abducted; if it fails, or past 6 months: usually closed or open reduction with a spica cast; untreated, a short leg and a painful hip</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Perthes disease</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Under 6: limited weight bearing and physical therapy; casting and bracing if refractory; 6 or older: femoral osteotomy; usually self-limited over 2 to 3 years, with later osteoarthritis</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Toddler&#x27;s fracture</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Immobilization and pain control; no further workup when the story fits</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Osgood-Schlatter disease</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Analgesics and rest</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"sp-dx\"><table class=\"dense\"><caption>Clues that change the answer</caption><thead><tr><th>Clue</th><th>Points to</th><th>Next move</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Knee pain with a normal knee exam</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Hip disease: Perthes disease or SCFE</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Examine and image the hip</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Morning stiffness easing through the day, 6 weeks or more</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Juvenile idiopathic arthritis<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"sjia\">Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis</button></div></span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Slit-lamp exam for uveitis; highest risk when antinuclear antibody is positive</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Migratory joint pain 2 to 4 weeks after a sore throat</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Acute rheumatic fever<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"arf\">Acute Rheumatic Fever</button></div></span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Antistreptolysin O titer and the Jones criteria</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>A nail through a shoe weeks ago, little fever</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Puncture-wound osteomyelitis<div class=\"sp-chips\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"sp-drw\" data-brief=\"bs-puncture-osteomyelitis\">Foot Puncture Wounds and Osteomyelitis</button></div></span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">MRI of the foot</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Adolescent athlete, pain below the knee, tender tibial tubercle</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Osgood-Schlatter disease</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Analgesics and rest</span></span></td></tr><tr><td>Breech girl, or a family history of DDH</td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">High risk for DDH</span></span></td><td><span><span class=\"mask\">Hip US at 6 weeks of age</span></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"sp-pause\"><b class=\"pp-t\">Pause point</b>\n<div class=\"pp-b\">\n<span class=\"sl\">Limping child</span>\n<span class=\"si pp-off\">Offered as <em>transient synovitis</em> or <em>growing pains</em></span>\n<span class=\"si\"><span class=\"mask\">Infection and leukemia not yet excluded</span></span>\n<span class=\"pp-a\">&rarr; <span class=\"mask\">Sick or night pain first</span></span>\n</div>\n<div class=\"pp-b\">\n<span class=\"sl\">Limp with knee pain</span>\n<span class=\"si pp-off\">Offered: <em>a knee film</em></span>\n<span class=\"si\"><span class=\"mask\">A normal knee exam</span></span>\n<span class=\"pp-a\">&rarr; <span class=\"mask\">Then the hip</span></span>\n</div>\n</div>\n\n<h5 class=\"authored-hdr\" id=\"lp-bank\">Practice</h5>\n<ol class=\"bank authored\">\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Toddler&#x27;s fracture\" data-d2=\"Septic arthritis\" data-item-id=\"q_bdee86ce98775e1e9a3f\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_09d766a4fd3758c8a2e5\" data-d1-id=\"o_baad0af6b8f65c6a8836\" data-d2-id=\"o_37885fd214105a699ac2\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Breech girl; painless limp, short leg, limited abduction\" data-d1-why=\"Painful, after a fall; hers is painless, with no tenderness\" data-d2-why=\"Fever and no weight bearing; she is afebrile\"><span class=\"sl\">13-month-old girl, walking for 1 month, with a painless limp</span>\n<span class=\"si\">born breech</span>\n<span class=\"si\">asymmetric thigh skin folds</span>\n<span class=\"si\">left leg shorter; left hip abduction limited</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Her mother wore a hip harness as an infant</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; no bruising or tenderness</span> &rarr; <b>Developmental dysplasia of the hip</b> &rarr; breech, female, a family history, a short leg and limited abduction in a new walker: missed DDH; past 4 months, image with a radiograph</li>\n<li data-type=\"test\" data-d1=\"Ultrasound of the hips\" data-d2=\"MRI of the pelvis and hips\" data-item-id=\"q_11abb36f6ad6ea3085a4\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_23af3d31e4d554d694f3\" data-d1-id=\"o_51195c25d04e0963d9a4\" data-d2-id=\"o_20b9a65960ca2918b6f1\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic study to obtain at this time?\" data-src=\"authored\" data-key-why=\"Past 4 months the head has ossified: radiograph\" data-d1-why=\"US is for infants under 4 months\" data-d2-why=\"Not the first test; the radiograph shows DDH\"><span class=\"sl\">2-year-old girl with a painless limp since she began walking</span>\n<span class=\"si\">right leg shorter than the left</span>\n<span class=\"si\">right hip abduction limited</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Born at term; no trauma; growth normal</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; knees and ankles normal</span> &rarr; <b>Radiograph of the pelvis</b> &rarr; missed DDH in a walker: the femoral head has ossified, so a radiograph; ultrasound is for infants under 4 months</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Transient synovitis\" data-d2=\"Osteomyelitis\" data-item-id=\"q_734cdaa7b585522dbb51\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_370295883daf5becaef1\" data-d1-id=\"o_81b610114e3e56f18c0e\" data-d2-id=\"o_c9f071752a925c41a48c\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"New refusal to bear weight after a fall; tibial tenderness\" data-d1-why=\"Days after a cold; his limp began after a fall and his pain is in the tibia\" data-d2-why=\"Fever and malaise; he is afebrile, CRP normal\"><span class=\"sl\">2-year-old boy who will not put weight on his right leg</span>\n<span class=\"si\">began yesterday after a fall at daycare that no one saw</span>\n<span class=\"si\">tender over the distal right tibia</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Well appearing</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; CRP normal &middot; hip and knee motion full</span> &rarr; <b>Toddler's fracture</b> &rarr; a new limp after a minor, unwitnessed fall with tibial tenderness: tibia radiographs for a spiral fracture; corner or bucket-handle fractures suggest abuse</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Transient synovitis\" data-d2=\"Slipped capital femoral epiphysis\" data-item-id=\"q_f4f48dfd36df5c08b6d9\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_c4671fe3c42a5910b8a6\" data-d1-id=\"o_56df3431eae15005a31b\" data-d2-id=\"o_62a48963b7c75a5bb4bf\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Boy of 6, limp for months; flat, fragmented head\" data-d1-why=\"Gone within about 2 weeks; this limp is 2 months old\" data-d2-why=\"Ages 10 to 15; the epiphysis slips, it does not fragment\"><span class=\"sl\">6-year-old boy with a limp for 2 months, worse after play</span>\n<span class=\"si\">intermittent groin and knee pain</span>\n<span class=\"si\">hip abduction and internal rotation limited</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">No fever, trauma or night pain</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; CBC and ESR normal &middot; radiograph: flattened, fragmented femoral head</span> &rarr; <b>Legg-Calvé-Perthes</b> &rarr; an insidious limp over weeks with normal labs at about 5 to 10 years; early films can be normal, so MRI when they are</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Legg-Calvé-Perthes\" data-d2=\"Transient synovitis\" data-item-id=\"q_879f0b60f4f452e09820\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_d055e1a472eb55279e9c\" data-d1-id=\"o_d48d0b40059b52f5b076\" data-d2-id=\"o_14ef0285d2485c34af05\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Age 13, growth spurt; hip rotates outward on flexion\" data-d1-why=\"Peaks at 5 to 7; a fragmented head, not a slip\" data-d2-why=\"Days after a cold; 3 weeks is too long\"><span class=\"sl\">13-year-old boy with 3 weeks of left thigh and knee pain and a limp</span>\n<span class=\"si\">95th percentile for height, 50th for weight</span>\n<span class=\"si\">left hip internal rotation lost; the hip rotates outward when flexed</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Knee examination normal</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; white cell count normal</span> &rarr; <b>Slipped capital femoral epiphysis</b> &rarr; lost internal rotation with obligate external rotation at the growth spurt, whatever the build: AP and frog-leg radiographs of both hips</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Reactive arthritis\" data-d2=\"Lyme arthritis\" data-item-id=\"q_33b90401a72c5bbfba10\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_faa35e6039915e369f7f\" data-d1-id=\"o_b65673d38829586fa766\" data-d2-id=\"o_d8d17081c98f552fb107\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Arthritis 6 weeks or more with morning stiffness\" data-d1-why=\"Follows diarrhea or urethritis; he had neither\" data-d2-why=\"Needs tick exposure; city child, no bites\"><span class=\"sl\">10-year-old boy with 8 weeks of a swollen right knee and a limp</span>\n<span class=\"si\">stiff each morning, easing by midday</span>\n<span class=\"si\">no diarrhea, urethritis or rash beforehand</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Lives in a city; no tick bites</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">T 37.2 °C &middot; ESR 28 mm/h</span> &rarr; <b>Juvenile idiopathic arthritis</b> &rarr; arthritis for 6 weeks or more with morning stiffness and no other cause; a slit-lamp eye exam screens for uveitis</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Juvenile idiopathic arthritis\" data-d2=\"Reactive arthritis\" data-item-id=\"q_069c75f1bef557f7b1bb\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_7e97930b08af5721bf93\" data-d1-id=\"o_4dfbe8dd285f5140a244\" data-d2-id=\"o_15a69f277cc85f148658\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Migratory arthritis 3 weeks after a sore throat; new murmur\" data-d1-why=\"Needs 6 weeks or more; this is 5 days and migratory\" data-d2-why=\"Follows gut or genital infection, not pharyngitis\"><span class=\"sl\">8-year-old boy with 5 days of painful joints and a limp</span>\n<span class=\"si\">left knee, then right ankle, then left elbow, one after another</span>\n<span class=\"si\">sore throat 3 weeks ago, untreated</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">New soft apical murmur</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">T 38.4 °C &middot; ESR 60 mm/h</span> &rarr; <b>Acute rheumatic fever</b> &rarr; migratory large-joint arthritis 2 to 4 weeks after pharyngitis: check an ASO titer and apply the Jones criteria; the murmur is carditis</li>\n<li data-type=\"test\" data-d1=\"Hip ultrasound for an effusion\" data-d2=\"Tibial radiographs\" data-item-id=\"q_c1f4667f9db45a88bc0d\" data-item-version=\"3\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_f3bd03f1d1305a1fa7f6\" data-d1-id=\"o_50efc14ca0c85cb7bb19\" data-d2-id=\"o_e141d5168c9951c2b2a5\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic study to obtain at this time?\" data-src=\"authored\" data-key-why=\"Night pain, bruising, pallor, weight loss: leukemia\" data-d1-why=\"Hip and knee motion are full; no effusion to find\" data-d2-why=\"For a well toddler after a fall; he looks ill\"><span class=\"sl\">5-year-old boy with 3 weeks of a limp</span>\n<span class=\"si\">leg pain that wakes him at night</span>\n<span class=\"si\">tired; bruises easily</span>\n<span class=\"si\">pale; lost weight this month</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">T 37.8 °C &middot; hip and knee motion full</span> &rarr; <b>CBC with smear</b> &rarr; a limp with night pain, bruising, fatigue or weight loss: CBC with smear for leukemia before calling anything benign</li>\n<li data-type=\"dx\" data-d1=\"Patellofemoral pain syndrome\" data-d2=\"Osteosarcoma of the proximal tibia\" data-item-id=\"q_18241f64b48e542aad5c\" data-item-version=\"2\" data-item-status=\"ready\" data-key-id=\"o_70b5b4cd7663508fbd84\" data-d1-id=\"o_e34647e2b0ee55ff9a9d\" data-d2-id=\"o_8b6cd219efc75fc8b508\" data-src=\"authored\" data-lead-in=\"Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\" data-key-why=\"Teen athlete; tender, prominent tibial tubercle\" data-d1-why=\"Pain around the patella, not at the tubercle\" data-d2-why=\"Night pain or weight loss; he has neither\"><span class=\"sl\">14-year-old boy with 3 months of anterior knee pain</span>\n<span class=\"si\">worse with running and jumping in basketball</span>\n<span class=\"si\">tender, prominent tibial tubercle</span>\n<span class=\"si\">hip motion full and painless</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">Afebrile &middot; no night pain or weight loss</span> &rarr; <b>Osgood-Schlatter disease</b> &rarr; the hip is cleared and the tibial tubercle is tender in an active adolescent: activity modification and nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div class=\"pearls nbq\" data-nbme=\"peds-cms-e52ce6\"><span class=\"lbl\">How NBME framed it: question 1</span><p class=\"nbstem\"><span class=\"sl\">Healthy 16-month-old girl with a painless right-sided limp since she began walking 4 months ago</span>\n<span class=\"si\">term; cesarean delivery for breech presentation</span>\n<span class=\"si\">no family history of musculoskeletal disorders</span>\n<span class=\"si\">left leg shorter than the right</span>\n<span class=\"si\">left hip abduction limited to 30 degrees; right hip normal</span>\n<span class=\"ss\">Sensation, circulation and strength normal</span></p><p class=\"nbask\">Most likely underlying mechanism?</p><span class=\"nbh\">Competing answers</span><ul><li><b>Acute epiphyseal fracture</b>: a toddler's limp; <span class=\"mask\">but it needs trauma, with pain, bruising and guarding; this limp is painless and 4 months old.</span></li><li><b>Acute suppurative arthritis</b>: limited hip motion; <span class=\"mask\">but it brings fever and pain; she is healthy and painless.</span></li><li><b>Avulsion of muscle origin</b>: a limp; <span class=\"mask\">but it follows an injury and hurts, often with a defect or mass.</span></li><li><b>Displacement of the capital femoral epiphysis</b>: limited hip motion and a limp; <span class=\"mask\">but that is the adolescent slip, which limits internal rotation.</span></li><li><b>Failure of osteoid to mineralize</b>: a toddler's leg problem; <span class=\"mask\">but rickets bows both legs; it does not shorten one or limit one hip.</span></li></ul><p class=\"two\"><b>Down to two:</b> poorly developed acetabulum or displacement of the capital femoral epiphysis. <span class=\"mask\">Poorly developed acetabulum: a breech girl with a painless limp from her first steps, a short leg and limited abduction has DDH missed in infancy. Every option was a disease written as its mechanism. (As in the source stem, the limp is on the right and the signs on the left.)</span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"pearls\"><span class=\"lbl\">Pairs with</span> <i>Septic Arthritis and Transient Synovitis in a Child</i>: fever, a toxic child or refusal to bear weight. <i>Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis</i>: age 10 to 15 with lost internal rotation. <i>Leg Pain in a Child</i>: leg pain with a normal gait. <i>Suspected Child Abuse</i>: a fracture the history does not explain, such as a corner fracture, or a femur fracture before walking. <i>Torticollis and Plagiocephaly in an Infant</i>: a breech infant also needs a hip exam.</div>\n\n<div class=\"rule\"><span class=\"lbl\">Transferable rule</span><p>Ask sick or well, then how old, then how long; a limp with night pain is a blood count first.</p></div>\n\n</div>",
   "ledger": "repair/migration/peds/hip/limp/spine/claim_map.json",
   "note": "s154c pause point blocks; shipped baseline preserved"
